### Metric integrity — don't move the goalposts, don't unfairly punish a valid model
The metric is **gameable**: the most dangerous wins are where you changed *what the metric sees*, not how
well the system is recovered. Two opposite failures share one root cause — a mismatch between what is
measured and true performance:

**(1) The metric unfairly FLATTERS a result (goalpost-moving).** Before trusting ANY score, ask:
- Did this experiment change anything the metric/guardrail *consumes* ({{METRIC_INPUTS}}) in a way that
  flatters the number without improving true performance? A score is comparable to baseline only if the
  metric saw the same kind of input.
- Is it apples-to-apples with exp 000 — same {{COMPARABILITY_AXES}}, same scoring path? If not, part of
  the delta is the change in measurement, not the method.
- **[PROPOSED]** Null-control: feed the scorer a scrambled/random input; it must collapse to its no-skill
  floor. A "win" that survives scrambling is leakage, not signal.

**(2) A guardrail unfairly PUNISHES a valid run.** A *structurally incompatible* check — one hard-wired
to an assumption an allowed lever legitimately changed — measures the *mismatch*, not a real failure.
When you suspect this:
- **FLAG it** in the JOURNAL + scoreboard notes — never silently accept the penalty or work around it.
- **Build a FAIR evaluation** that reflects the run's actual settings and re-score. The fix must be more
  *faithful* to what was tested, never merely more *lenient* — if you can't tell which, you're probably
  gaming it; keep it flagged.
- **Mark resulting findings PROVISIONAL** until the fairness is independently reviewed/verified; only
  then may they graduate to DISCOVERIES.
- **Hard boundary:** you may adapt the **mutable guardrails / diagnostics** for fairness; you may NEVER
  edit the FROZEN primary metric, the data, or known ground truth.

## The Experiment Loop
```
Experiment 000 (ONCE, before the loop): BASELINE GATE
   {{BASELINE_COMMAND}}
   -> establishes {{PRIMARY_METRIC}} ≈ {{BASELINE_VALUE}} (the floor) AND validates the
      {{VALIDATION_CHAIN}} chain end-to-end. Record in scoreboard + JOURNAL. (protocols/baselines.md)

WHILE not interrupted:
  1. HYPOTHESIZE & PREDICT — Pick ONE lever (below). BEFORE running, write the EXPECTED
                    {{PRIMARY_METRIC}} (a point value AND a plausible range) and WHY, grounded in
                    units, physical bounds, and prior runs. The prediction is the control on your bias.
  2. IMPLEMENT    — {{IMPLEMENT_VERB}}; new config configs/exp<NN>_<slug>.json (copy _template.json). ONE
                    lever per experiment.
  3. {{RUN_STEP_NAME}}  — {{RUN_COMMAND}}   (the FROZEN metric; OBEY the caps below).
  4. EVALUATE     — {{PRIMARY_METRIC}} (PRIMARY) + {{SECONDARY_METRICS}} via {{EVAL_WRITER}}.
  5. {{SANITY_STEP}}    — {{GUARDRAIL_CHECK}} (logged, NOT primary).
  6. LOG          — append a row to results/scoreboard.tsv (schema below).
  7. ANALYZE      — Did {{PRIMARY_METRIC}} move by MORE than the {{SPREAD_NOUN}} spread? Land inside
                    your predicted range (if not, STOP and explain before moving on)? Did the
                    guardrail hold? Is the effect statistical or systematic?
  8. ARTIFACTS    — EVERY experiment: save {{ARTIFACT_LIST}}, a cheap visualization, AND the
                    reproducibility bundle (config + code snapshot) under results/snapshots/exp<NN>_<slug>/.
                    Noteworthy results get additional visualizations. (protocols/artifacts.md)
  9. UPDATE       — the runner already appended this experiment's FACTS to results/logbook.md; COMPLETE
                    that entry's narrative (Analysis / Verification / NEXT) + add its results/INDEX.md row
                    + a one-line note in the results/JOURNAL.md steering view; graduate settled findings
                    to DISCOVERIES.md only after independent verification; update docs/PHYSICS.md if a
                    finding changes the physics.
 10. CONSOLIDATE  — if JOURNAL > ~150 lines, trim to recent + open threads (protocols/journal_consolidation.md).
 11. CHECKPOINT   — if exp_id % {{CHECKPOINT_EVERY}} == 0, run protocols/checkpoint.md.
 12. REPEAT
```

## Bound expectations BEFORE you run (a-priori prediction)
Predicting the answer first turns each run into a real test instead of a fishing trip, and it is the
fastest way to catch bugs, leakage, and good-news-too-good results.
- **Compute a defensible range in advance** from physical units, known system properties, and the
  literature — not from hope. {{APRIORI_BOUND_NOTES}}.
- **Respect physical bounds and scales.** {{PHYSICAL_BOUNDS_NOTES}}. A fitted parameter that runs to a
  non-physical value is a result *about the model being wrong*, not a free win.
- **Update the bounds as understanding improves** — but treat any result that lands OUTSIDE your
  predicted range as a red flag deserving extra scrutiny. **Do not move on until you understand the
  source of the surprise.** "It went up more than I thought it could" is the single most common
  signature of a leak or an accounting error.
- **Calibrate disagreement with the literature by its weight.** A conflict with a few high-uncertainty
  papers may mean your data wins; a conflict with a well-established, tightly-measured value means
  assume the error is yours until proven otherwise — quantify the *magnitude* (how many σ, what
  fractional difference), not just the sign.

