## The levers (what the agent CAN change — change ONE per experiment)
{{LEVERS_LIST}}

Roughly orthogonal families: {{LEVER_CATEGORIES}}. After a plateau, escalate to an ORTHOGONAL lever —
do not keep tweaking one knob.

### Choosing among levers and models (parsimony + tie-breaking)
- **Could another model/parameterization explain the data equally well?** If two options fit
  comparably they are degenerate on this metric — prefer the simpler, and design a discriminating test
  rather than declaring a winner by a coin-flip-sized margin.
- **When two options tie within the spread, break the tie deliberately** on the cost that matters
  here: {{TIEBREAK_COSTS}} (computational time, parameter complexity, robustness across conditions,
  interpretability). Don't add a knob you can't justify.
- **A freer model that wins only by adding flexibility is the gap-to-ceiling trap.** Extra capacity
  that closes the gap without raising the ceiling is usually overfitting.

### Idea sources — novel ideas welcome, cite the borrowed ones
You are explicitly **encouraged to pursue well-founded novel ideas that go beyond the published
literature** — the campaign is not limited to known approaches. When a hypothesis DOES come from a
specific source (a paper, a doc, the user), **cite that source** on the JOURNAL `Hypothesis` line (and in
`LITERATURE.md` if it exists). Tracking where each idea came from is part of the record.

## FROZEN / out of scope (NEVER change; NEVER ask for)
- **FROZEN** (no edit without re-validation + user sign-off): the scoring math `{{FROZEN_SCORER_PATH}}`;
  {{FROZEN_LIST}}; the experimental data (read-only).
- **READ-ONLY** (use, don't edit): {{READONLY_LIST}}.
- **MUTABLE**: {{MUTABLE_LIST}} (+ `configs/` — a new file per experiment).

Do NOT pause to request more data, a different metric, or a radically different objective. Work
strictly within the levers above.

