## Hardware safety & irreversibility
- **Respect hardware limits as HARD constraints.** {{HARDWARE_LIMITS}}. A setpoint outside the safe
  range is out of scope, never a lever — never exceed it to chase a number.
- **Confirm before any hard-to-reverse or destructive action** (anything that could damage the sample
  or instrument, or consume a limited resource). Hardware runs are expensive and often irreversible —
  plan each one; you cannot cheaply re-run. Pre-screen candidates with a forward model / digital twin
  where one exists.
- **Beat drift: interleave/randomize comparisons** (A-B-A) rather than all-A-then-all-B, so calibration
  or condition drift is not read as an effect; keep key parameters fixed and fair between compared runs.
- **Rule out measurement artifacts before claiming a real effect** — re-check calibration and baseline
  first. The primary metric carries statistical AND systematic error; never act on a single read.
- **Log the full instrument + environment state every run** (settings, calibration, firmware,
  conditions — the "thermostat in the box").
