## Independent verification — assume the result is WRONG and try to REFUTE it
Convergence is not correctness. Before any finding is graduated to DISCOVERIES.md or accepted as
current-best, it must be independently re-derived. **The campaign's verification plan
(`docs/VERIFICATION_PLAN.md`, or the tailored `SIMULATION_VALIDATION.md` for sim-heavy work) lists the
application-specific checks decided at setup** — `verify` reads them; `correctness-suite` encodes the
checkable ones as standing regression gates.
- **Validate every fit even when it converged cleanly** — re-run from the config, recompute the metric
  from scratch, confirm it reproduces within tolerance.
- **Falsify with code, not prose, and confirm the numbers are real.** Write verification scripts and
  controls (edge cases, randomized inputs, counterexamples) rather than arguing in text; and confirm
  every reported number was actually produced by the evaluation — re-run it (e.g. re-run inference on the
  test set), never trust a number that was only stated.
- **Spawn an independent agent to refute it.** Use the **`{{VERIFY_SKILL}}`** skill: it dispatches a
  fresh agent instructed to *assume the result is wrong* — re-runs the experiment, recomputes
  `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}` and the guardrail, replicates with perturbed params, checks for metric
  contamination{{VERIFY_EXTRA_CHECKS}}, and returns CONFIRMED / PROVISIONAL / QUESTIONABLE ASSUMPTION /
  REFUTED / INCONCLUSIVE
  with evidence. A promotion is not real until this returns CONFIRMED. Run that verifier on a model **at
  least as capable** as the loop — a *different* capable model is ideal (more mechanism diversity), **never
  weaker** (a weak verifier yields false CONFIRMs). Mechanical sub-tasks the loop delegates — search,
  citation checks, figure generation, validation — can run on a lighter model, since a deterministic gate
  or your own read catches a slip.
- **Scale verification to the run cost.** When an experiment is **fast**, reproduce it fully and add
  multiple seeds + a statistical test. When it is **long/expensive** and a full re-run isn't practical,
  the verifier instead scrutinizes the **code and the conclusions/logic** and re-checks the **saved
  artifacts** (models / data / logs) rather than re-running from scratch — which is why those artifacts
  must be saved (see "Where everything is saved"). The verify skill adapts its depth to the runtime.
- **Verify the FAIRNESS of any adapted evaluation.** If a result relied on an adapted guardrail (see
  "The opposite failure" above), the verifier must judge whether the adaptation was *faithful* or
  merely *lenient*. Such findings stay PROVISIONAL until that judgment returns "faithful".
- **Record disputes — append-only.** Log every issue the verifier (or the user) raises in the
  experiment's JOURNAL `Disputes & responses` line, each with your response. **Never delete a raised
  issue; rebut it if you disagree.** These records are part of the project's scientific integrity.

## Rigor — replicate before you conclude
- **Replicate with perturbed parameters AND, for any stochastic step, multiple seeds** to tell a real,
  robust effect from run-to-run noise. A change smaller than the replicate spread is not a result.
- **Could the effect be a seed/init artifact?** Confirm with multiple seeds/inits before ANY hard
  conclusion, positive or negative.
- **Track error and separate its kinds.** Report each metric with a spread; distinguish *statistical*
  error (shrinks with more seeds/frames — run more) from *systematic* error (a convention, pose, or
  calibration bias — more runs won't help; find and fix the cause). {{SYSTEMATIC_AXIS_NOTE}}
- **Use a statistical test, not eyeballing, for close calls.** Compare per-{{UNIT}} distributions
  (paired) rather than two point means; "different" must clear the spread.
- **[PROPOSED] Define and report effect size.** State the minimum meaningful Δ`{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}`
  (≥ 2× the replicate std) up front, and report deltas as `Δ ± spread`.
- **This is REQUIRED before** (a) graduating a finding to DISCOVERIES.md, or (b) permanently writing
  off an approach. One run never justifies either.

## Decision-making guidelines
- **Primary up, guardrail holds** → log, queue a confirmation / seed-repeat.
- **Only {{CHEAP_SUBGROUP}} improved** → cheap/near-trivial; not a real win, keep exploring.
- **Primary up, guardrail degraded** → suspect {{OVERFIT_MODE}}; second seed / control.
- **Flat/ambiguous** → after 2–3 inconclusive scouts, move to an ORTHOGONAL lever.
