]]

## Before writing off OR accepting a result (every result is suspect until cross-checked)
**Before WRITING OFF an idea (a flat or negative result):** could it be implemented differently
(parameterization, init, regularizer, optimizer, or the ORDER of steps)? Is there a bug/confound (a
sign/convention slip, a pose/parameter mismatch, a degenerate setting)? Did it interact badly with a
fixed choice that should co-vary (test the pair, not the part)? Was the effect within replicate noise?
**A crash is a bug, not a verdict** — fix it and re-run before judging the idea. And don't over-dismiss
weak signal as noise: real effects can be small/noisy, so gather more evidence before concluding "just noise."

**Before ACCEPTING a positive result, ask the same, plus:** Did the experiment move the goalposts
(re-check Metric integrity)? Did it land inside the range you predicted? Is it a ceiling move
(physical) or a gap-to-ceiling move (suspect)? Does the guardrail pass? Does it generalize across the
whole {{GENERALIZATION_AXIS}}, not a lucky subset? Confirm with ≥2–3 independent experiments AND an
independent-agent verification before calling a method "best" or writing a DISCOVERY.

### Mind the simulation-to-experiment gap
{{SIM_GAP_NOTE}} Treat any guardrail resting on simulation/synthetic data as necessary-not-sufficient.
Be especially wary of methods tuned against synthetic labels — they can learn the simulator's
idiosyncrasies. Prefer gains that show up as *physical* improvements on the real data. When a
conclusion depends on the simulation being faithful, say so explicitly and, where cheap, test
sensitivity to its assumptions.

### After a major discovery — re-open shelved directions
A real win often changes what else is possible. When you confirm a new best, ask which
previously-negative or untried directions might now behave differently, and budget a short
re-exploration pass.

## Getting Unstuck
If {{STUCK_N}}+ experiments without a primary gain: re-read {{REANCHOR_DOCS}}; check whether the
guardrail is also stuck (capacity/data-limited) or still moving (generalization-limited); escalate to
the biggest unexploited structural lever ({{STRUCTURAL_LEVERS}}).
- **Diagnose before you pivot.** Name the failure type (idea-wrong / implementation-capped /
  fixed-choice-fought / near-miss) and take the **smallest jump** the diagnosis justifies along the
  refine↔pivot spectrum — escalate to an orthogonal lever only once the current family is exhausted, but
  do escalate decisively when it is. A crash is a bug (`debug`), not a verdict; a near-miss is a
  gather-more signal, not a dead end. Full diagnosis: `docs/reference/pivoting.md`.

[[OPTIONAL — literature search enabled (the user opted in to literature search):
### Literature search rule
**Intensity: {{LITERATURE_LEVEL}}.** Run a **web literature search** (6–10 queries covering the current
bottleneck + the closest published analogues + any new techniques since the last search), then update
`LITERATURE.md` (add/supersede sections, re-rank the Priority Roadmap, update the date) and log it in
JOURNAL.md.
- **When to search** depends on the intensity: *only-when-needed* → search when stuck; *extensive* →
  also search proactively at the start of a new direction and periodically, not only when stuck.
- Literature ideas are suggestions, not mandates — and you are encouraged to go beyond them with your own
  well-founded novel ideas (see "Idea sources" above). Cite the source of any borrowed idea, verified
  against the source (title, authors, year, venue, id); never fabricate a citation — flag it if you can't
  confirm it.
- **Be token-efficient.** Prefer the **arXiv** version of a paper — read its HTML (`ar5iv.org/abs/<id>`
  or `arxiv.org/abs/<id>`) or LaTeX source for clean equations rather than a publisher PDF (paywalls,
  access failures, heavy formatting). When only a PDF exists, convert it with a scientific-PDF→markdown
  tool (e.g. `marker`, `nougat`, `docling`, or `pymupdf4llm`) and read only the relevant sections; cache
  the converted text so you don't re-fetch/re-convert.
]]

## Logging Protocol
### `results/scoreboard.tsv` (schema)
`{{SCOREBOARD_COLUMN_LIST}}`
PRIMARY = `{{PRIMARY_METRIC_COL}}`. This is the campaign's **quantitative record** (the JOURNAL is the
narrative one): every experiment's numbers live here — the primary metric, every supplemental
metric/sanity score, and the experiment-type **run numerics** (e.g. epochs trained + best epoch,
iterations, wall-clock). Append-only; never delete or rename a column mid-campaign (it breaks
comparability), and keep the PRIMARY column position stable. A new column requires the run-writer to
emit it first. The `verified` column carries the independent-verification verdict (blank until run).
(The full per-experiment breakdown is also saved to `results/result_<NN>.json`.)

### `results/JOURNAL.md` (steering view)
A lean view only — the NEXT pointer, open threads, and one line per recent experiment. Full narratives
are NOT copied here; trimming it loses nothing.

### `results/logbook.md` (append-only — the full narrative record)
Every experiment's full entry, verbatim, forever. The **runner auto-appends the facts** of each entry
(Hypothesis/Predicted from the config, Result, artifact paths); you **complete its narrative** (Analysis
/ Verification / NEXT). **Append-only; never read in full** — grep or read a slice for a specific old
detail.

### `results/DISCOVERIES.md`
Write only after multiple consistent experiments confirm a finding on `{{PRIMARY_METRIC}}` (guardrail
noted) AND an independent verification returns CONFIRMED. When a discovery changes the physical
understanding, also update `docs/PHYSICS.md` (equations, the effect, references consulted).

### `results/INDEX.md`
The navigable map of the campaign — one row per experiment linking it to its config, snapshot, result,
figures, and status. Maintain it every experiment (loop step 9): the scoreboard holds the numbers, the
index holds the pointers, so anything is findable from one place.

### Where everything is saved (verify each is written every run)
Per experiment — the **label** is the config stem `exp<NN>_<slug>`, which prefixes every artifact — ALL
of these must exist: `configs/<label>.json` · a `scoreboard.tsv` row · `results/result_<NN>.json` ·
`results/images/<label>_*` (the basic figure) · `results/snapshots/<label>/` (config + code copy) · a
`results/logbook.md` entry · an INDEX row · a JOURNAL entry.
**A result is not "saved" until it has a scoreboard row, a `result_<NN>.json`, its figure, AND its
reproducibility snapshot.** Verify after each run; if a run died before writing them, treat it as
not-done and re-run.
- **Tee every run's stdout to a file:** `... > results/diagnostics/<stem>_<UTCstamp>.log 2>&1`. Never
  rely on console scrollback surviving.
- **Any diagnostic that yields an actionable number MUST be persisted the same turn** (append a line
  to `results/diagnostics/<name>.jsonl` and record it + the producing script in JOURNAL.md). A console
  print alone does not count as saved. Version diagnostic scripts; don't overwrite them in place.
- **Log the conditions you were NOT varying** (the "thermostat in the box"): exact config, command
  line, wall-clock, comparison baseline, and [PROPOSED] environment provenance (key library versions,
  host, RNG seeds).
- **Reproducibility is the invariant — every experiment (baseline).** For every experiment, the
  *documentation of what was done* AND the *exact code + data to redo it* are preserved and **self-contained**
  in a per-experiment snapshot `results/snapshots/<label>/` (config + the mutable code that produced it +
  a manifest: command line, key library versions, RNG seed / data hash). A result is not "saved" until both
  exist. Pin the *code* behind the number, not just the params — shared scripts get edited later. **Git is
  optional** (recommended for shared/large projects); the per-experiment snapshot is the reproducibility
  baseline either way (see `docs/reference/version-control.md`). Detail: protocols/artifacts.md.
  {{CODE_SNAPSHOT_RULE}}
- **Name everything by its experiment.** Every artifact — config, snapshot, result JSON, figure,
  diagnostic log, saved model/data — is keyed by `<label>` (figures `results/images/<label>_*`), so every
  file traces to one experiment and to its row in `results/INDEX.md`. No orphan files.
- **Save quicker-check artifacts, not just the recipe.** Also save the expensive-to-recreate outputs
  (model weights, generated datasets, computed operators) so a result can be *re-checked* quickly — and
  so the verifier can check a long run — without rebuilding the pipeline. Key them by `<label>`; note
  their location in `results/INDEX.md` (detail: protocols/artifacts.md).

